{
  "framing": "The proposal: release notes should be derived from the merged change record rather than written after the fact, so the notes cannot drift from what actually shipped; the author would rather adopt an established convention or tool than invent a format, and has not chosen either yet. A repo check supports exploring this now: Nexus has no release tags and no changelog today, so there is nothing to retrofit; the merge history mixes three conventions (bare summaries, lower-case prefixes such as 'docs:', and 'HARN-<n>:' keys) with conventional merge-commit lines, and no written commit or PR-title convention exists in the project docs. One observation that seems to bear on the idea: every merged change here is tied to a Jira task whose key, summary and description are already normalized and human-written, and PR titles tend to carry that key — so the richest record of intent may already exist before the merge. Also worth naming, without it being a defect: if notes are derived strictly from the merge record with no later writing at all, they will report what changed but rarely why it mattered; whether the notes are a faithful enumeration or need a light judgment layer is a choice the refinement can settle.",
  "questions": [
    "Who reads these notes and what decision or check do they support — Nexus's own operators and contributors, or the users of projects Nexus manages? That shapes how much of the Jira context belongs in a release note.",
    "What precisely is 'the merged change record' for one release: pull request titles and descriptions, commit subjects, the Jira tasks those changes completed, or a combination with a stated precedence? Today's data suggests the Jira summary is the most normalized human text available.",
    "Where and when should the notes appear — in the repository at release time, attached to a version or tag, or published somewhere else? And should anything still be written by hand at any point, or is the intent that the release step is fully mechanical?",
    "The author's premise is written-afterwards notes that drift; existing tools split into two families — some (Conventional Commits plus generators) demand a strict machine-readable convention, others (Changesets, release-drafter) capture a human summary at merge time and assemble the notes automatically. Which trade-off fits better: tightening future convention, or drawing on the task record already produced by the workflow?"
  ],
  "authorDecision": null
}

